```java
ConcurrentHashMap<String, Session> sessions = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

// safe: each call is independently atomic
sessions.put(sessionId, newSession);
Session s = sessions.get(sessionId);
sessions.remove(sessionId);
```

Each of these three calls is safe on its own, from any number of threads, with no external locking. `ConcurrentHashMap` guarantees no corrupted internal state and no lost writes across concurrent single-key operations — this is exactly the case it was built for.

```java
// BROKEN: two calls, not one atomic operation
if (!sessions.containsKey(id)) {
    sessions.put(id, createSession());   // another thread can insert here first
}
```

Both `containsKey` and `put` are individually thread-safe, but the sequence between them is not — two threads can both see `containsKey` return false, and both call `put`, with the second silently overwriting the first's session. "Concurrent" describes the collection's own internals, not the safety of whatever your code does across two of its calls.

```java
// FIXED: one atomic call instead of check-then-act
sessions.computeIfAbsent(id, key -> createSession());
```

`computeIfAbsent` performs the check and the insert as a single atomic operation inside the map's own implementation — no other thread can observe or act in a gap that no longer exists in your code. When no built-in compound method matches the exact operation you need, the fallback is an external lock around the whole sequence, accepting that you're no longer relying on the collection's own atomicity for that part.
